{
  "openapi": "3.0.3",
  "info": {
    "title": "issuedesk API \u2014 API-key endpoints",
    "version": "0.1.0",
    "description": "The subset of the issuedesk API that an **API key** can call.\n\nAPI keys are read-only \u2014 they may call any `GET` endpoint \u2014 **with three families of exceptions**:\n\n- **Comments** \u2014 post (`POST \u2026/comments`), edit (`PATCH \u2026/comments/{id}`) and delete (`DELETE \u2026/comments/{id}`).\n- **Attachments** \u2014 list and upload (`GET`/`POST \u2026/attachments`).\n- **Ticket description** \u2014 replace it (`PATCH /api/tickets/{slug}/description`). This is a dedicated sub-path so a key can rewrite a ticket body without being able to change status, assignee, priority or type.\n\nEverything else (sign-in, creating/deleting issues, changing status or assignment, user and key management) is reserved for the browser app and is not shown here.\n\n## Authenticating\n\nSend your key as `X-API-Key: idk_\u2026` (or `Authorization: Bearer idk_\u2026`). API keys are exempt from the browser-lock, so curl/agents need no extra headers. A key acts on behalf of the user who created it, so any comment it posts is attributed to that user; it may edit or delete its own comments (and, as it carries admin rights, any other comment).\n\nThe same endpoints also work from the signed-in browser app via a JWT bearer token."
  },
  "servers": [
    {
      "url": "/",
      "description": "This host"
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    {
      "name": "tickets",
      "description": "Relay endpoints addressed by public KEY-number \u2014 the primary surface for API-key clients"
    },
    {
      "name": "projects",
      "description": "Read project metadata"
    },
    {
      "name": "issues",
      "description": "Read issues"
    },
    {
      "name": "comments",
      "description": "Read, post, edit and delete comments"
    },
    {
      "name": "stats",
      "description": "Read dashboard figures"
    }
  ],
